3. Compiling and installing Websh (Unix)
----------------------------------------

Requirements:
- Tcl 8.6 or newer (threaded build)
- GNU make

  cd src/unix
  autoconf            # required: configure is not in the repository (generated files are not tracked)
  ./configure --with-tcl=/path/to/tcl/lib
  make
  make test           # expected: 497 passed, 5 skipped, 0 failed
  make install

Build targets (VERSION as set in configure.ac, e.g. 3.7.9; shared
library extension is .so on Linux, .dylib on macOS):
- webshVERSION          standalone binary (dynamically linked to libwebsh)
- libwebshVERSION.so    TEA-compliant shared library (Tcl package "websh")
- mod_webshVERSION.so   Apache module (built automatically when headers found)

Install layout (prefix = /usr/local/tcl86):
- bin/webshVERSION, bin/websh -> webshVERSION
- lib/webshVERSION/libwebshVERSION.so, lib/webshVERSION/pkgIndex.tcl
- lib/libwebshVERSION.so -> webshVERSION/libwebshVERSION.so
- lib/libwebsh.so -> libwebshVERSION.so
- lib/httpd/modules/mod_webshVERSION.so  (Apache only)
- lib/httpd/modules/mod_websh.so -> mod_webshVERSION.so  (Apache only)

The configuration and the demo material go to the major.minor line
(SERIES = 3.7 for any 3.7.x), so that WebshConfig in the Apache
configuration survives a patch release untouched:
- share/webshSERIES/conf/websh.conf.dist   always written (fresh delivery)
- share/webshSERIES/conf/websh.conf        only created when absent
- share/webshSERIES/conf/*.ws3, httpd.conf, htdocs/, README

Since that directory holds the file Apache actually reads, make install
never overwrites an existing websh.conf: the new delivery is installed
next to it as websh.conf.dist, and a difference between the two is
reported. Compare them with diff and carry over what you want.

The code itself stays fully versioned (bin, lib, mod_websh): the Tcl
package version and the library name the Apache module was linked
against are part of the contract.

On Linux, make install automatically runs ldconfig and restarts Apache
(systemctl restart apache2 / httpd) if the service exists. On macOS
with Homebrew httpd, make install restarts Apache via brew services.

To build without the Apache module:

  ./configure --with-tcl=/usr/local/tcl86/lib --disable-apache

To force the Apache module (fail if headers are missing):

  ./configure --with-tcl=/usr/local/tcl86/lib --enable-apache

APR and APU include paths are detected automatically via apr-1-config
and apu-1-config. Override if needed:

  ./configure --with-tcl=/usr/local/tcl86/lib \
      --with-aprinclude=/path/to/apr/include

On macOS with Homebrew, Apache and APR paths are found automatically
for both Intel (/usr/local) and Apple Silicon (/opt/homebrew) — no
extra flags required.

To change the install prefix:

  ./configure --with-tcl=/usr/local/tcl86/lib --prefix=/opt

The shipped websh.conf starts with an "encoding system" line, utf-8 by
default. It is process-global in Tcl, so it reaches every request
interpreter although websh.conf runs in its own -- without it the
encoding comes from the locale of the Apache process, which makes the
same application behave differently from host to host. To choose a
different encoding or leave the line out:

  ./configure --with-system-encoding=iso8859-1
  ./configure --without-system-encoding

The name is validated against the Tcl being built against, so a typo
such as "utf8" fails configure instead of taking down every .ws3 of the
server when Apache starts.


4. mod_websh: Websh as Apache Module
------------------------------------

Websh applications can run in CGI mode or as mod_websh, the Apache 2.4
module. mod_websh is built automatically when Apache headers are found.

Note: mod_websh is not compatible with mod_http2. Disable mod_http2 in
the Apache configuration when using mod_websh.

mod_websh manages a pool of Tcl interpreters to handle concurrent
requests efficiently. Refer to doc/mod_websh/README for configuration
details.


